{% section 'lbass-collection-hero' %}
{% comment %}
  Keep the complete collection in one decision/filter surface. The previous
  24-item page split made a 48-item collection look incomplete and meant the
  client-side filters could only search half the catalogue. Shopify supports
  up to 250 items per paginate page; product images remain lazy-loaded.
{% endcomment %}
{% paginate collections[collection_source_handle].products by 250 %}
{%- comment -%}
  ═══ GRID SOURCE — MUST BE collection.products (2026-08-26) ═══

  The grid used to loop `collections[collection_source_handle].products`, i.e.
  the collection looked up BY HANDLE. That lookup always returns the collection's
  DEFAULT order and the FULL product set — it silently ignores both the URL's tag
  filter and its ?sort_by=. Measured live before this fix:

      /collections/track-pants          -> 9 unique products
      /collections/track-pants/black    -> 9   (identical)
      /collections/track-pants/red      -> 9   (identical)
      /collections/all?sort_by=best-selling -> byte-identical order to default

  So every tag-filtered URL rendered the unfiltered grid. That broke the
  shop-by-colour chips in snippets/lbass-colour-nav.liquid, which link to
  /collections/<handle>/<colour> — a shopper clicking «حمر» got every colour.

  `collection.products` is the filtered, sorted view Shopify builds from the URL.
  Use it for the GRID. The only exception is the audited-drop page, which
  deliberately renders the whole catalogue.

  Note the chip/vendor lists below now reflect the filtered view too, which is
  correct — they describe what is actually on screen. The colour nav keeps its
  own unfiltered lookup by handle on purpose, so every colour stays reachable
  from a filtered page; do not "align" it with this.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  audited-drop deliberately shows the WHOLE catalogue instead of just its own
  members — but only while nothing is filtered. Before 2026-09-04 the override
  was unconditional, so on that page the grid rendered collections.all.products
  no matter what: applying أبيض showed the count drop to 10 while all 64 products
  stayed on screen. Any active filter (tag, list value, or price) now falls back
  to the real filtered view.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign lb_any_filter = false -%}
{%- if current_tags != blank -%}{%- assign lb_any_filter = true -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- for f in collection.filters -%}
  {%- if f.active_values.size > 0 -%}{%- assign lb_any_filter = true -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if f.type == 'price_range' -%}
    {%- if f.min_value.value -%}{%- assign lb_any_filter = true -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- if f.max_value.value -%}{%- assign lb_any_filter = true -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{% if collection.handle == 'audited-drop-p001-p012' and lb_any_filter == false %}
  {% assign collection_product_source = collections.all.products %}
{% else %}
  {% assign collection_product_source = collection.products %}
{% endif %}
{%- comment -%}
  ═══ IN-STOCK FIRST (2026-08-26) ═══
  Sold-out pieces used to take the opening slots of the grid purely on their
  sort position. Measured 2026-08-26, /collections/all opened with
  adidas-black-3-stripes-track-pants, which has 0 inventory — the first thing a
  visitor saw could not be bought. Every product here is one-of-one, so sold-out
  is permanent, not a restock away.

  Available pieces are floated to the front while sold-out ones stay on the page
  (they are still useful proof that stock genuinely moves, and they keep their
  URLs alive for anything already linking to them). Order within each group is
  untouched, so the collection's own sort order still applies.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% assign _in_stock = collection_product_source | where: 'available', true %}
{% assign _sold_out = collection_product_source | where: 'available', false %}
{% assign collection_product_source = _in_stock | concat: _sold_out %}
{% assign collection_product_ids = ',' %}
{% for collection_item in collection.products %}{% assign collection_product_ids = collection_product_ids | append: collection_item.id | append: ',' %}{% endfor %}
